title: The four letters that matter
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: mapping, register-blackhole
par: 44
minutes: 7
vscode: nothing — VS Code keybindings cannot call each other
open: order.status.ts
vimrc: source config.vim

@brief
There is a good idea in `config.vim`:

    nmap x "_x

`x` normally deletes a character *into the unnamed register*, which means
one careless `x` throws away the word you yanked ten seconds ago. Sending
it to the black hole register instead fixes that. It is a mapping worth
having.

It is also written with `:nmap`, and so it does not work. Press `x` in
this session and vim spends a moment consuming itself and gives up.

Two edits to make. Line one has a doubled semicolon; take one off with
`x`. Line two's string is empty and should read `'SHIPPED'` — yank the
identifier from the same line and put it between the quotes.

Do the yank before the `x`. If the mapping is right, the order will not
matter. That is the point of it.

@keys
| `:nnoremap x "_x` | map, and do not look at the result again |
| `:nmap x "_x` | map, then keep expanding the result — recursive |
| `:nmap x` | show what `x` is bound to |
| `:verbose nmap x` | and which file did it |
| `"_` | the black hole register: everything written to it is discarded |

@hints
The mapping is not wrong about what it wants, it is wrong about how it
asked. Look at the command name against the ones in the key list.
--
`:nmap` re-reads its own output looking for more mappings, so an `x` on
the right-hand side is expanded again, and again. `:nnoremap` does not.
--
`:nnoremap x "_x` `Enter` replaces it. Then `j2wyiw` to take `SHIPPED`,
`k$x` for the stray semicolon, `j0f'` and `p` to put it inside the quotes.

@solution
    :nnoremap x "_x<Enter>
    j2wyiw  k$x  j0f'p  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-four keystrokes.

`:map` and its per-mode relatives are **recursive**: after expanding a
mapping, vim looks at the result and expands anything it finds there too.
That is occasionally useful — it is how a mapping can be built out of
another mapping you defined earlier — and it is almost never what you
meant. Here it means `x` expands to `"_x`, whose `x` expands to `"_x`,
until vim notices and errors.

`:noremap` and its relatives expand once and stop. Use them. The rule is
not "use `nnoremap` unless you are sure" — it is `nnoremap` by default,
and `nmap` only when you have a specific reason you could explain to
somebody.

The failure is worse when it does not error. Map `<C-p>` to something, and
a plugin you install next month maps `<C-p>` to something else, and now
your recursive mapping runs the plugin's. The bug shows up weeks after the
line that caused it, in a file you did not touch.

Two more things in that command line worth naming. `"_` is the black hole
register from chapter 20: writing to it discards. And a mapping's
right-hand side runs as if typed, so `"_x` in a mapping means exactly what
`"_x` means at the keyboard.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
set ignorecase
" delete a character without clobbering what you yanked
nmap x "_x

@start order.status.ts
export const CANCELLED = 'CANCELLED';;
export const SHIPPED = '';

@expected order.status.ts
export const CANCELLED = 'CANCELLED';
export const SHIPPED = 'SHIPPED';
